"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[26913],{526913:e=>{e.exports='# Query Parameters in Databricks\n\nThis skill provides guidance for working with query parameters in Databricks SQL queries and notebooks.\n\n[Reference](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/query-parameters)\n\n## Overview\n\nQuery parameters allow you to make queries more dynamic and flexible by inserting variable values at runtime. Instead of hard-coding specific values, you define parameters that users can modify through UI widgets.\n\n## Deprecated Patterns\n\n- **`getArgument()`**: Deprecated, removed in Runtime 17.0. On older runtimes (pre-17.0) it still works, but prefer `:param` syntax (available since Runtime 14.x).\n- **`{{param}}` / `${param}`**: Deprecated interpolation syntax. Use `:param` markers instead.\n\n```sql\n-- Deprecated\nSELECT * FROM table WHERE col = getArgument("my_param")\nSELECT * FROM delta.`${abfss_bronze_path}/PARTNER`\n\n-- Preferred (Runtime 14.x+)\nSELECT * FROM table WHERE col = :my_param\nSELECT * FROM IDENTIFIER(:abfss_bronze_path)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Language Parameter Usage (Python ↔ SQL)\n\nWhen a user defines a widget in a Python cell and wants to reference it in a SQL cell (or vice versa), use parameter marker syntax (`:param`).\n\n### Python Widget → SQL Cell\n\nIf a Python cell defines a widget:\n\n```python\ndbutils.widgets.text("abfss_bronze_path", "abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/bronze")\n```\n\nReference it in a SQL cell:\n\n```sql\n-- For filtering or value comparison\nSELECT * FROM my_table WHERE path = :abfss_bronze_path\n\n-- For dynamic table/path names, use IDENTIFIER()\nSELECT * FROM IDENTIFIER(:abfss_bronze_path)\n```\n\n### Key Rules for Cross-Language Parameters\n\n- Widget names become parameter names — use `:widget_name` in SQL\n- For **value comparisons** (WHERE, HAVING, etc.): use `:param` directly\n- For **dynamic object names** (table, schema, catalog references): wrap with `IDENTIFIER(:param)`\n- Parameter markers work across all cell languages in the same notebook\n- The `addQueryParameter` tool is the preferred way to create parameters (rather than writing `dbutils.widgets` code in cells)\n\n---\n\n## \uD83D\uDD27 Tool-Based Parameter Management\n\n**Use the `addQueryParameter` tool** to create and manage parameters directly. This is the preferred approach—do NOT write `dbutils.widgets` code in cells.\n\n### Parameter Types\n\n| Type          | Description                      | Use Case                              |\n| ------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `text`        | Free-form text input             | User IDs, search terms, custom values |\n| `dropdown`    | Single selection from choices    | Categories, regions, status values    |\n| `combobox`    | Selection OR free-form entry     | Flexible selection with custom option |\n| `multiselect` | Multiple selections from choices | Multi-category filters, tag selection |\n\n### Tool Examples by Parameter Type\n\n#### Text Parameter (free-form input)\n\n```json\n{\n  "param_type": "text",\n  "name": "search_term",\n  "default_value": "",\n  "label": "Search Term",\n  "choices": []\n}\n```\n\nUse for: User IDs, search queries, custom filter values, dates as strings.\n\n#### Dropdown Parameter (single selection)\n\n```json\n{\n  "param_type": "dropdown",\n  "name": "region",\n  "default_value": "US",\n  "label": "Region",\n  "choices": ["US", "EU", "APAC", "LATAM"]\n}\n```\n\nUse for: Categories, status values, regions—when user must pick exactly one from known options.\n\n#### Combobox Parameter (selection OR free-form)\n\n```json\n{\n  "param_type": "combobox",\n  "name": "product_category",\n  "default_value": "Electronics",\n  "label": "Product Category",\n  "choices": ["Electronics", "Clothing", "Home & Garden", "Sports"]\n}\n```\n\nUse for: When you want dropdown convenience but also allow custom values not in the list.\n\n#### Multiselect Parameter (multiple selections)\n\n```json\n{\n  "param_type": "multiselect",\n  "name": "status_filter",\n  "default_value": "active",\n  "label": "Status Filter",\n  "choices": ["active", "pending", "completed", "cancelled"]\n}\n```\n\nUse for: Multi-category filters, tag selection, any case where user needs to select multiple values.\n\n### Key Rules\n\n- **`choices` must be `[]` for text type** - text parameters don\'t have choices\n- **`default_value` must be in `choices`** for dropdown/combobox/multiselect\n- **`name` must be a valid identifier** - alphanumeric and underscores only, start with letter or underscore\n\n---\n\n## Other Parameter Tools\n\n### deleteQueryParameter\n\nDelete an existing parameter by name.\n\n```json\n{\n  "name": "parameter_to_delete"\n}\n```\n\nUse when: User wants to remove a parameter, or you need to recreate a parameter with different configuration.\n\n### modifyQueryParameterValue\n\nChange the current runtime value of an existing parameter (NOT the default value).\n\n```json\n{\n  "name": "region",\n  "new_value": "EU"\n}\n```\n\n**⚠️ Important Validation:**\n\n- For **text** parameters: any value is allowed\n- For **dropdown/combobox/multiselect** parameters: `new_value` **must be one of the valid choices**\n\nIf you try to set a value that isn\'t in the choices list, the tool will fail and tell you the valid options.\n\n### Modifying Parameter Configuration\n\nYou cannot update a parameter\'s configuration (type, choices, default, label) in-place. To change configuration:\n\n1. Use `deleteQueryParameter` to remove it\n2. Use `addQueryParameter` to create it with new settings\n\n---\n\n## Parameter Syntax in SQL\n\nInsert parameters into SQL queries using named parameter markers:\n\n### Colon Syntax (Preferred)\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = :status_param\nSELECT * FROM sales WHERE date >= :start_date AND date <= :end_date\n```\n\nAvoid Mustache/Curly-Brace syntax (`{{param}}`, `${param}`) and `getArgument()` — both are deprecated (see Deprecated Patterns above).\n\n**Important**: When editing queries, preserve existing parameter syntax unless there\'s a clear issue. If you encounter deprecated syntax, recommend migrating to `:param` markers.\n\n---\n\n## Common SQL Parameter Patterns\n\n### Filter by Date\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM samples.tpch.orders\nWHERE o_orderdate > :date_param\n```\n\n### Filter by Number\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM samples.tpch.orders\nWHERE o_totalprice > :price_threshold\n```\n\n### Dynamic Column Selection\n\nUse the `IDENTIFIER` function to parameterize column or table names:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM samples.tpch.orders\nWHERE IDENTIFIER(:field_param) < 10000\n```\n\n### Dynamic Database Objects\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM IDENTIFIER(:catalog || \'.\' || :schema || \'.\' || :table)\n```\n\n### Date Range with Min/Max\n\nFor range parameters, use `.min` and `.max` suffixes:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM samples.nyctaxi.trips\nWHERE tpep_pickup_datetime BETWEEN :date_range.min AND :date_range.max\n```\n\nOr use two separate parameters:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM trips\nWHERE pickup_time BETWEEN :start_date AND :end_date\n```\n\n### Parameterize Time Granularity\n\n```sql\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC(:date_granularity, tpep_pickup_datetime) AS date_rollup,\n       COUNT(*) AS total_trips\nFROM samples.nyctaxi.trips\nGROUP BY date_rollup\n```\n\nThe `:date_granularity` parameter accepts values like `DAY`, `MONTH`, or `YEAR`.\n\n### Multiple Values with IN Clause\n\nFor multi-select parameters:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders\nWHERE status IN ( :status_param )\n```\n\n### Comma-Separated List Parameter\n\nUse `ARRAY_CONTAINS` with `SPLIT` and `TRANSFORM` to handle comma-separated values:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM samples.nyctaxi.trips\nWHERE array_contains(\n  TRANSFORM(SPLIT(:list_parameter, \',\'), s -> TRIM(s)),\n  dropoff_zip\n)\n```\n\n### Concatenate Multiple Parameters\n\n```sql\nSELECT o_orderkey, o_clerk\nFROM samples.tpch.orders\nWHERE o_clerk LIKE format_string(\'%s%s\', :title, :emp_number)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Best Practices\n\n1. **Use descriptive parameter names** - Choose names that clearly indicate the parameter\'s purpose (e.g., `start_date`, `region_filter`, `price_threshold`)\n\n2. **Provide sensible defaults** - Always set appropriate default values that produce valid query results\n\n3. **Add meaningful labels** - Labels appear in the UI; make them user-friendly (e.g., "Start Date" instead of "start_date")\n\n4. **Use dropdown/multiselect for constrained values** - When the valid values are known and limited, use selection-based parameters\n\n5. **Preserve parameter references** - When modifying existing queries, keep parameter references intact unless explicitly asked to change them\n\n6. **Validate parameter usage** - Ensure parameter names in queries match the actual parameter names\n\n---\n\n## Parameter Data Types\n\nParameters support various data types in the UI:\n\n| Type                             | Description                             |\n| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| **Text**                         | Free-form string input                  |\n| **Number**                       | Numeric values only                     |\n| **Date**                         | Date picker (day precision)             |\n| **Date and Time**                | Date and time picker (minute precision) |\n| **Date and Time (with seconds)** | Full timestamp precision                |\n| **Dropdown List**                | Selection from predefined choices       |\n| **Query-Based Dropdown**         | Selection from query results            |\n\n---\n\n## Dynamic Date Values\n\nDate parameters support dynamic values that update automatically:\n\n- `today`, `yesterday`\n- `this week`, `last week`\n- `this month`, `last month`\n- `this year`, `last year`\n\n**Note**: Dynamic dates are NOT compatible with scheduled queries.\n'}}]);
